      <h2>The Advanced Dead Hang Level</h2>

      <p>Advanced dead hang training on this site starts at a controlled 60-second overhand hold. That is this site's own starting point and not a published standard. Once bodyweight hangs feel easy, external load and one-arm work are the usual next steps.</p>

      <p>Two common goals at this stage are a longer bodyweight hang and a one-arm hang. Neither has a published standard. Track your own holds. The doses on this page are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

      <p>Advanced training also needs careful recovery. <a href="/injury-prevention/">Injury prevention</a> matters as much as the training because the fingers, wrists and elbows work under higher loads.</p>

      <h2>Weighted Dead Hang Protocol</h2>

      <p>Weighted dead hangs add external load through a dip belt, weight vest or dumbbell between the feet. A dip belt with chain and carabiner is a practical option for progressive loading.</p>

      <h3>Getting Started</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Starting weight:</strong> +5 to 10 lbs (2 to 5 kg) even if you feel you can do more</li>
        <li><strong>Sets:</strong> 3-4 per session</li>
        <li><strong>Hold time:</strong> 15-30 seconds per set</li>
        <li><strong>Rest:</strong> 2-3 minutes between sets</li>
        <li><strong>Progression:</strong> Add about 5 lbs once every set is clean</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Loading Progression</h3>

      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
