      <p>The loads in this table are examples, not requirements. Use the lightest load that makes the target time hard. Adding weight quickly can overload the fingers and elbows. The <a href="/weighted-deadhang/">weighted dead hang guide</a> sets the prerequisite and the loading rules.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Key rule:</strong> If finger joint pain or elbow tenderness lasts beyond a day, stop weighted hangs and see a clinician before resuming.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>One-Arm Dead Hang Protocol</h2>

      <p>In a one-arm dead hang each arm carries about twice the load it carries in a two-arm hang. The shoulder also has to stay stable. A progressive approach lowers the chance of injury. A controlled two-arm hang of about 60 seconds is the starting prerequisite. This is a conservative guide, not a tested threshold.</p>

      <h3>Assisted One-Arm Progression</h3>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Towel-assisted hang:</strong> Grip the bar with one hand. The other hand grips a towel draped over the bar and takes part of the load. Hold 8 to 12 seconds per arm.</li>
        <li><strong>Finger-assisted hang:</strong> Grip the bar with one hand. Place 2-3 fingers of the other hand on the bar for minimal support. Hold 10 to 15 seconds per arm.</li>
        <li><strong>Full one-arm hang:</strong> Grip the bar with one hand only. The free arm hangs by your side or rests on your thigh. Target 5 to 15 seconds per arm.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>One-Arm Training Rules</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>Train the weaker arm first when grip is fresh</li>
        <li>Alternate arms between sets, not mid-set</li>
        <li>Rest 90-120 seconds between sets</li>
        <li>Perform 3 or 4 sets per arm on non-consecutive days</li>
        <li>Log each arm separately to track imbalances</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Most people grip a little harder with one hand; about 10% is the figure usually quoted for right-handers, with little difference in left-handers. The <a href="/one-arm-deadhang/">one-arm dead hang guide</a> has the full build-up.</p>

      <h2>Towel and Fat Grip Training</h2>

      <p>Increasing the diameter of your grip surface makes the hold harder. Two common methods are towel hangs and fat grip attachments.</p>

      <h3>Towel Hang</h3>

      <p>Drape a thick gym towel over the bar. Grip the towel ends with both hands. The soft, thick surface forces your fingers to work harder. Hold times are usually shorter on a towel than on a bar. The amount varies between people. Start with 10 to 15 second holds and build from there. The <a href="/towel-hang/">towel hang guide</a> covers setup.</p>

      <h3>Fat Grip Attachments</h3>

      <p>Rubber sleeves that clip onto a standard bar make the grip thicker than the usual 28 to 32 mm (about 1.1 to 1.25 inches). Thicker bars are harder to hold. Use less load or shorter holds. Use fat grips for 1 or 2 sessions per week as an accessory to standard bar training.</p>

      <h3>Training Integration</h3>

      <p>The grip tool table shows example targets, not standards.</p>

      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
