      <h2>Grip Training for Sport</h2>

      <p>Some people use dead hangs as supplementary grip work for sport. Each sport also demands specific skills beyond general grip endurance.</p>

      <h3>Climbers</h3>

      <p>Climbing demands finger strength on small holds and sustained grip across long routes. Hangboard work on small edges is a different skill with its own injury risk to the finger pulleys. Follow a published protocol and a qualified coach. Bar hang time is not a validated predictor of climbing endurance.</p>

      <h3>Powerlifters</h3>

      <p>For many lifters grip is what ends a heavy deadlift. Some add weighted hangs of 15 to 20 seconds on days separate from deadlift sessions. Some hang with a mixed grip to practice the position they pull with.</p>

      <h3>Strongman Athletes</h3>

      <p>Strongman events demand both crushing grip (stone loading) and support grip (farmer's walks). Dead hangs train support grip. Pair them with fat grip hangs and towel hangs for thick-handle support grip. Vary the grip tool across the week.</p>

      <h2>Sample Advanced Week</h2>

      <p>This example week has three harder sessions with a rest day between them and one optional light session. It mixes weighted, one-arm, endurance and active hangs. It is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

