      <h2>Recovery and Deload</h2>

      <p>Advanced grip training puts a lot of stress on connective tissue. Tendons adapt more slowly than muscle. Raise load gradually and plan recovery so overuse does not derail your training.</p>

      <h3>Elbow Care</h3>

      <p>Inner elbow tenderness and pain when gripping are early warning signs of elbow overload. Rest from hanging and see a physiotherapist before starting rehab exercises if elbow pain persists.</p>

      <h3>Skin Care</h3>

      <p>Thick calluses tear under heavy load. File calluses smooth after showering when the skin is soft to reduce tearing. Keep calluses present but thin. Apply hand balm to prevent dry cracking. Torn skin needs rest from hanging until it heals.</p>

      <h3>Deload Protocol</h3>

      <p>A lighter week about every fourth week is a common coaching convention, not a tested rule. In a lighter week cut volume and load and keep to about three sessions. Skip max-effort attempts.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Recovery rule:</strong> Hold times that keep falling across several sessions suggest you need more rest. Take a lighter week.</p>
      </div>

